[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry[/b][/size]
[b]Unpublished 2.2.10 description draft.[/b] The next private pair is CDT 2.2.10-outputs.1 / CrimsonHue 1.0.8-outputs.1. Package validation is pending and no live Tuya acceptance is claimed. Published CDT 2.2.9 / Hue 1.0.7 still requires Hue setup for OpenRGB and has no Tuya output. This text is prepared only; it has not been uploaded to Nexus.

[b]Live light, player, camera and ambient data, with all-around light capture, a 3D radar, fullscreen markers and local HTTP/WebSocket APIs.[/b]

Telemetry reads current light contributions before the renderer's view selection, including sources behind the camera. Optional physics queries estimate collision on camera-to-light paths. Raw data remains available independently of that estimate.

[b]Validated reference: Crimson Desert Steam build 26423880 on Windows, EXE 1.0.0.3527.[/b] Guarded automatic recovery supports compatible address changes; changed engine layouts or semantics can still require a mod update.

[size=4][b]Upcoming independent lighting outputs[/b][/size]
The matching candidate's Setup page selects Hue, OpenRGB and Tuya LAN independently. OpenRGB-only and Tuya-only need no Hue hardware. Fresh setups select no output; existing saved Hue pairing keeps Hue enabled. Common Start/Stop controls configured selected outputs, and a failed output does not cancel healthy outputs. CDT hosts the panel; CrimsonHue owns the output connections and settings.

The new Tuya LAN page supports compatible Wi-Fi protocol 3.5 RGB lamps with individual room positions, shared tuning and menu lighting. Add/Edit opens a Windows dialog; connection data and local keys are stored together in a current-user DPAPI-protected file and never enter CDT's shared HUD. Check connection tests TCP reachability only. Protocol 3.3/3.4, Zigbee and Bluetooth are not supported by this backend.

Tuya starts at a 25% output cap, sends changed colors no faster than every 300 ms per lamp and keeps only the latest desired color, with 2.5-second failure backoff. Stop restores a supported queried prior state only after confirming current ownership; otherwise the last lamp state can remain. These are implementation limits, not measured physical performance. [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonHue/blob/main/docs/USER-GUIDE.md#independent-output-candidate-unpublished]Candidate setup and Tuya limits[/url].

[size=4][b]Included since 2.2.9: simulation pause and menu lighting[/b][/size]
After 500 ms without simulation progress, CDT pauses new light, ambient and visibility acquisition and managed pose/camera sampling. An independent observer keeps running; resume requires fresh captures and discards old capture/smoothing history. The new activity API reports running, paused or unknown. Missing or stale observers become unknown.

The independent-output candidate requires the separately provided CrimsonHue 1.0.8-outputs.1 companion; the future public target is 1.0.8. Published downloads remain [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonHue/releases]on GitHub[/url]. [b]CDT works independently; CrimsonHue is optional and is not included in the CDT ZIP.[/b]

CDT draws the mixer in its existing D3D12 renderer. CrimsonHue owns bridge communication, protected settings, spatial calibration and lighting output, without a second graphics-hook set. Existing raw light data and routes remain compatible; activity is a separate API.

[list]
[*][b]Steady menu light:[/b] in Lighting > Menu lighting, select brightness and color/hex, then Save menu lighting. Hue and enabled OpenRGB devices use this color while paused and blend back over 500 ms once fresh gameplay data returns.[/*]
[*][b]Preserved choices:[/b] Reset menu lighting restores warm white at 20%; Gold preset is an optional choice. Existing settings are not changed automatically. Overall brightness and OpenRGB output limits still apply.[/*]
[*][b]OpenRGB reset and cleanup:[/b] Reset OpenRGB settings disables OpenRGB, restores its defaults and moves current devices to the default front position while preserving the SDK port and Hue settings. Remove disconnected devices preserves connected placements and output settings. Both require stopped sync; cleanup also requires a fresh Read devices result. Reset view changes only the view angle.[/*]
[*][b]Quiet pause/resume:[/b] intentional pauses and pending fresh post-resume captures no longer produce light-reader error notices or repeat Telemetry is ready. Startup, actual scene loads, genuine errors and compatibility recovery retain their notifications.[/*]
[/list]

[b]This detects simulation pauses, not individual menu IDs.[/b] Other simulation stalls can also trigger it. The reported controller-only Skills-menu stutter and rapid hover sounds remain unresolved; this release does not claim to fix them. The production INI enables [Pause] Enabled=1; use 0 to disable this policy. The monitor-only diagnostic is off.

[size=4][b]Optional lighting mixer[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Lighting / Room / Setup / Advanced:[/b] bridge and Entertainment-area setup, ordinary and segmented wall-light calibration, Recommended/Custom controls, presets and diagnostic data.[/*]
[*][b]Lighting weights:[/b] Point lights 0-200%, Spotlights 0-1000%, Own lantern 0-100%. Point/spot is independent of standalone/grouped contributions. Own-lantern matching uses a conservative complete-pair signature for the supported build; missing or ambiguous matches keep ordinary type weighting.[/*]
[*][b]Consistent hiding:[/b] Ctrl+Shift+H, Hide settings and title-bar X hide the mixer while lighting sync continues. Use Stop to end sync, and wait for stopped status before game exit.[/*]
[*][b]Input handoff:[/b] held delivered game presses finish releasing before panel capture; panel-owned releases finish on hiding. Raw mouse motion has a fallback, and the duplicate native cursor is suppressed inside the owned foreground game client. Release held keys/buttons if prompted.[/*]
[*][b]Room controls:[/b] Point/Wall setup, per-segment wall-washer calibration including horizontal arrangements, and sharper Recommended spatial defaults. Refresh changed Entertainment layouts before restarting sync.[/*]
[/list]

Input handoff has mocked-input/shared-renderer coverage; not every independently polled or buffered game-input route has been proven. Neither module starts physical lighting just by loading. In the candidate, OpenRGB is disabled by default and requires a local SDK server; a Hue bridge is needed only for Hue output.

[size=4][b]Telemetry features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]All-around light capture:[/b] current engine lights remain available beyond the camera view. Particle groups are decoded into summed contributions; one physical fire can still produce several contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Light data:[/b] world position, linear HDR RGB, luminance, recognized spotlight direction/cone and renderer-selection metadata.[/*]
[*][b]Physics collision checks:[/b] clear, blocked or unknown, with ray counts and measurement age. Enabled by default.[/*]
[*][b]Three light feeds:[/b] raw contributions, grouped/smoothed contributions, and a clear-only grouped/smoothed feed. Raw records and RGB are preserved.[/*]
[*][b]Player and camera:[/b] player position/root orientation plus independent native render-camera position, orientation and projection.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient/sky:[/b] global sky RGB, optional camera-local sky visibility and a derived local ambient estimate. Local fields can be null.[/*]
[*][b]HUD and API:[/b] fullscreen markers, player-centered 3D radar, notices, local HTTP snapshots/health/schema and WebSocket streams.[/*]
[*][b]Independent activity feed:[/b] /v1/activity and /v1/activity/stream expose simulation state, observer age, acquisition pause and session/epoch information using schema 1.0. The normal snapshot/light schema remains 1.6.[/*]
[*][b]SDR/HDR UI:[/b] SDR, HDR10 and scRGB support. Automated rendering tests are separate from live display/game acceptance.[/*]
[/list]

The 2.2.4 sky-readback and world-entry queue corrections remain included.

[url=https://youtu.be/eyRkkTXAU64]Watch the telemetry and HUD demonstration[/url]
The video and screenshot show an earlier version; they do not demonstrate the current menu-light controls.
